= The Modeling Problem
#text(
  size: 8pt,
  fill: luma(45%),
  style: "italic",
)[Primary sources: Murphy (2023), Chapter 10; Ganguly and Earp (2021), _An Introduction to Variational Inference_.]

A latent-variable model describes observations $x$ using unobserved variables $z$:
$
  p_theta (x, z)=p_theta (z)p_theta (x|z).
$ <joint-model>
The model runs _forward_: sample a latent explanation from the prior, then generate an observation from the likelihood. Inference reasons in the opposite direction. After observing $x$, we want
$
  p_theta (z|x)=(p_theta (x, z))/(p_theta (x)),
  quad p_theta (x)=integral p_theta (x, z)dif z.
$ <posterior>
The joint is often easy to evaluate at one configuration even when the evidence requires summing or integrating over an enormous set. Exact inference exploits special graph structure but becomes impractical in dense or high-dimensional models. MCMC approximates expectations with posterior samples and can represent complicated posteriors, but may be expensive. _Variational inference_ (VI) turns inference into optimization: choose a tractable family $cal(Q)$ and find a member near the posterior.

#def(
  term: "Variational inference",
)[For a fixed generative model, choose an approximate posterior $q in cal(Q)$ and optimize it to be close to $p_theta (z|x)$. The result depends separately on the model, the family, and the optimization procedure.]

== Running model: a Bayesian Gaussian mixture

Let $x_(1:N)$ be scalar observations generated by one of $K$ components. We use global latent component means $mu_(1:K)$ and one local latent assignment $c_i$ per observation:
$
          mu_k & ∼ cal(N)(0,tau^2), quad k=1,dots,K, \
           c_i & ∼ "Cat"(1/K,dots,1/K), \
  x_i|c_i=k,mu & ∼ cal(N)(mu_k,1).
$ <mixture-model>
The joint is
$
  p(x,mu,c)=product_(k=1)^K p(mu_k)
  product_(i=1)^N p(c_i)p(x_i|c_i,mu).
$ <mixture-joint>
The means are _global_: every observation informs them. Assignments are _local_: $c_i$ belongs to observation $i$. Even with fixed hyperparameters, exact posterior inference must reason over $K^N$ assignment configurations while coupling assignments to uncertain means.

