= The Evidence Lower Bound

Assume the displayed expectations are finite and $q$ has no mass outside the posterior support. Expand reverse KL using Bayes' rule:
$
  D_("KL")(q(z)||p_theta (z|x))
  &=EE_(q)[log q(z)-log p_theta (z|x)]\
  &=EE_(q)[log q(z)-log p_theta (x, z)]+log p_theta (x).
$
Define the _evidence lower bound_ (ELBO)
$
  cal(L)(theta,q;x)
  eq.def EE_(q)[log p_theta (x, z)-log q(z)].
$ <elbo>
Then
$
  underbrace(log p_theta (x), "log evidence")
  =underbrace(cal(L)(theta,q;x), "ELBO")
  +underbrace(D_("KL")(q(z)||p_theta (z|x)), "inference gap").
$ <elbo-identity>
Because KL is nonnegative, $cal(L)<=log p_theta (x)$. For fixed $theta$, maximizing the ELBO over $q$ is equivalent to minimizing reverse KL. Equality holds exactly when the posterior belongs to the family and is attained.

Two equivalent forms explain what is optimized:
$
  cal(L) & =EE_(q)[log p_theta (x, z)]+H(q), \
         & =EE_(q)[log p_theta (x|z)]-D_("KL")(q(z)||p_theta (z)).
$ <elbo-forms>
The first balances high joint density with variational entropy. The second is expected data fit minus departure from the prior. It is not two unrelated losses.

