= Mean-Field Coordinate Ascent

Hold all factors except $q_j$ fixed. Let $q_(-j)=product_(ell != j)q_ell$. Terms involving $q_j$ can be written
$
  cal(L)(q_j)
  =-D_("KL")(q_j (z_j)||tilde(p)_j (z_j))+"constant",
$
where
$
  tilde(p)_j (z_j) prop exp(EE_(q_(-j))[log p_theta (x, z)]).
$
Thus the free-form coordinate optimum is
$
  q_j^* (z_j) prop exp(EE_(q_(-j))[log p_theta (x, z)]).
$ <cavi>
Read @cavi as: _take the expected log joint, exponentiate, normalize_. It is not the expectation of the joint and is not generally obtained by plugging in means. Only log-joint factors that touch $z_j$ survive, connecting CAVI directly to Markov blankets.

An exact sequential coordinate update cannot decrease the ELBO. The complete mean-field objective may still be nonconvex, so initialization can matter and convergence is not a global-optimum certificate.

== Assignment update in the mixture

For assignment $c_i$, keep only its likelihood term. Under $q_k (mu_k)$,
$
  EE[(x_i-mu_k)^2]=(x_i-m_k)^2+s_k^2.
$
Therefore
$
  log r_(i k)
  ="constant"+x_i m_k-1/2(m_k^2+s_k^2),
$ <responsibility-update>
so normalize over components:
$
  r_(i k) prop exp(x_i m_k-1/2(m_k^2+s_k^2)).
$
The update rewards components whose inferred means fit $x_i$, but penalizes uncertain components through $s_k^2$. Replacing $EE[mu_k^2]$ by $m_k^2$ would erase that uncertainty.

== Component-mean update

Collect the Gaussian prior and all likelihood terms weighted by responsibilities. Completing the square gives
$
  s_k^2 & =(tau^(-2)+sum_i r_(i k))^(-1), \
    m_k & =s_k^2 sum_i r_(i k)x_i.
$ <mean-update>
The effective count $sum_i r_(i k)$ increases posterior precision. The posterior mean is a responsibility-weighted data sum shrunk toward the prior mean zero.

#colorbox(title: "CAVI for the Bayesian mixture", color: blue.darken(10%))[
  + Initialize $(m,s^2,r)$, preferably more than once.
  + Update every responsibility vector $r_i$ with @responsibility-update and normalize.
  + Update every component factor $(m_k,s_k^2)$ with @mean-update.
  + Compute the ELBO and stop when its improvement is small.

  Label symmetry gives equivalent solutions with permuted component names. Poor initialization can also produce inferior local optima.
]

