= Inference, Learning, and Scale

The phrase “optimize the ELBO” hides two different tasks.

- *Posterior inference:* hold model parameters $theta$ fixed and optimize $q$. The evidence is constant in $q$.
- *Model learning:* optimize $theta$ as well. The evidence changes with $theta$.
- *Variational EM:* alternate an approximate E-step, maximizing $cal(L)$ over $q$, and an M-step, maximizing it over $theta$.
- *Variational Bayes:* place priors on model parameters and include their posterior approximation inside $q$; the mixture above treats $mu$ this way.

These distinctions matter when interpreting the mixture. Updating $q(mu_k)$ represents uncertainty about a Bayesian parameter. Updating a point parameter $theta$ changes the generative model. Both may use the same ELBO, but they answer different questions.

== Minibatches and global/local structure

For a global latent $beta$, local latents $z_n$, and product family $q(beta)product_n q_n (z_n)$, write the ELBO as a global term $G$ plus local terms $ell_n$. A uniformly sampled minibatch $cal(B)$ of size $B$ gives
$
  hat(cal(L))=G+N/B sum_(n in cal(B))ell_n.
$ <minibatch>
Scale the sampled local sum, not the single global prior or entropy term. This rule follows from the global/local modeling decomposition, not from a generic instruction to multiply every minibatch loss by $N/B$.

