= Transfer to Neural Latent-Variable Models

A VAE uses the same modeling and inference objects:
$
  p(z)p_theta (x|z),
  quad q_phi (z|x).
$ <vae-model>
The decoder $p_theta (x|z)$ is the generative model. The encoder $q_phi (z|x)$ is an _amortized inference procedure_: instead of optimizing separate variational parameters for every observation, a shared network predicts them. Training jointly learns a model and an inference mechanism.

For a diagonal Gaussian encoder,
$
  q_phi (z|x)=cal(N)(z|mu_phi (x),"diag"(sigma_phi (x)^2)),
$
reparameterize
$
  epsilon∼cal(N)(0,I),
  quad z=mu_phi (x)+sigma_phi (x)dot.o epsilon.
$ <reparam>
The base noise is independent of $phi$, exposing a differentiable path through the sample. A one-sample objective is
$
  hat(cal(L))=log p_theta (x|z)-D_("KL")(q_phi (z|x)||p(z)).
$
For the standard VAE prior $p(z)=cal(N)(0,I)$, the KL is analytic:
$
  D_("KL")=1/2 sum_j (mu_j^2+sigma_j^2-1-log sigma_j^2).
$

== Reconstruction is not generation

A reconstruction samples $z$ from $q_phi (z|x)$ and decodes it. Generation samples $z$ from the prior $p(z)$ and then samples from $p_theta (x|z)$. The encoder is not needed to generate from the learned model. Good reconstruction alone does not establish good density modeling, calibrated inference, or useful latent structure.

Local VI optimizes each case separately. Amortized VI is fast at deployment but may miss the best member of its family. Semi-amortized VI initializes from the encoder and refines locally. Amortization changes _how_ a family member is selected; it does not repair dependence that the family cannot represent. Hard discrete samples generally need exact summation, another gradient estimator, or a relaxation rather than this ordinary Gaussian pathwise construction.

